    "titleZh": "吞掉信件的保存按钮",
    "summary": "A new postal terminal has a button marked Save, and every sender assumes a letter that disappears has begun its journey.",
    "summaryZh": "新邮亭里有一个写着“保存”的按钮，寄信人每次按下它，都以为信已经上路。",
    "storyParagraphs": [
      "The city introduced electronic postal terminals. After composing a letter, users saw one prominent Save button. Pressing it made the letter disappear and returned the screen to a blank page. Many took this to mean the letter had been sent.",
      "In reality, Save placed the message in a private drafts folder. Sending required another menu. Designers understood the internal structure and considered the two steps clear. Ordinary users relied on experience with paper mail: once a letter left the desk, the post office had it.",
      "After a month, birthday letters and payment notices remained in drafts. Instructions had been posted, but few people read them before acting. The explanation users had formed continued to conflict with the machine's behaviour, creating repeated error.",
      "The designers separated the actions visibly. Saving kept the message on screen with the label 'Visible only to you'. Sending used a paper-plane symbol and then displayed recipient, time, and 'Accepted by post'. Leaving with an unsent message triggered a clear warning.",
      "The next sender did not walk away after pressing Send. She waited until the recipient's name appeared before taking her hand from the terminal."
    ],
    "storyParagraphsZh": [
      "城里启用电子邮亭。写完信后，屏幕上只有一个显眼的“保存”按钮。寄信人按下以后，信件从画面消失，机器又回到空白页。许多人据此相信信已经被送走。",
      "实际上，“保存”只把信放进私人草稿箱，真正发送还要打开另一层菜单。系统设计者熟悉内部结构，觉得两步十分清楚；普通人却依据纸信经验判断：信一旦离开桌面，就已经交给邮局。",
      "一个月后，大量生日信和付款通知仍躺在草稿箱。说明书早已张贴，却很少有人在操作前阅读。用户形成的解释与机器真实行为持续错位，错误便一次次重复。",
      "设计者把两个动作分开呈现：保存后，信仍留在画面并标记“仅自己可见”；发送按钮使用纸飞机图标，按下后显示收件人、时间和“已交付邮局”。离开页面前，未发送的信还会得到明确提醒。",
      "下一位寄信人按下发送后，没有立即走开。她等到屏幕出现收件人姓名，才把手从机器上移开。"
    ],
    "conceptName": "Mental models in human-computer interaction",
    "conceptNameZh": "人机交互中的心智模型",
    "explanationParagraphs": [
      "A mental model in human-computer interaction is the explanation users form from experience, cues, feedback, and learning to predict system behaviour; it need not mirror implementation. Labels, mappings, constraints, state visibility, and feedback shape it. When the presented model repeatedly conflicts with real behaviour, documentation alone rarely fixes systematic error."
    ],
    "explanationParagraphsZh": [
      "人机交互中的心智模型是用户依据经验、线索、反馈和学习形成的系统解释，用来预测操作结果，不必等同底层实现。设计通过标签、映射、约束、状态可见性和反馈塑造模型。若呈现持续违背真实行为，单靠说明书难以修复系统性错误。"
    ],
    "mode": "concept-fable",
    "status": "approved",
    "qualityGate": {
      "reviewRounds": 3,
      "rewriteRounds": 2,
      "openCritical": 0,
      "openMajor": 0
    },
