    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "After the spring fair, the inn yard was piled with paper cups discarded after one use. The owner, Madam Qin, called them wasteful and immediately ordered five hundred thick ceramic cups from another province, each stamped 'the lasting choice' underneath. Their delivery required three heavy carts, and forty broke on the journey. She had not counted the firing of the cups or the wood and water needed to wash them each day.\n\nThe first month was worse. Travellers carried cups away without thinking, servers broke a batch, and the washroom heated water all day. The paper merchant said ceramic used more resources; the potter pointed to the rubbish pile and said paper had to be remade every day. They argued at the counter, each describing only the part of the journey that favoured his own goods.\n\nThe bookkeeper placed a pot of tea on the table. 'Are we comparing one cup,' he asked, 'or the serving of one hundred pots of tea?' Beginning with clay digging and tree cutting, he recorded manufacture, transport, use, washing, number of returns, and final destination. He kept fuel, water, breakage, and recovery in separate columns.\n\nNo cup won by its nature alone. If a ceramic cup was used twice, the burden of firing and transport was difficult to recover. With a small deposit, efficient shared washing, and dozens of uses, its cost per serving fell. Madam Qin used ceramic for guests staying at the inn. At the takeaway stall, where returns were uncertain, she kept light paper cups and required the supplier to collect them.\n\nAt the end of summer, a traveller returned a ceramic cup and reclaimed his deposit. Madam Qin turned the cup over, carved one more mark beside the thirty-six fine scratches already on its base, and placed it back in the basin of hot water.",
