    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "Wheat ears at Stone Slope Farm shortened for three years. Owner Gu Rong bought more fertilizer each season and spread it all before spring planting, certain that anything missing from the soil could simply be added. Across the farm, cattle manure stood uncovered beside a ditch, most straw was burned after harvest, and carts of grain and milk continually left the gate.\n\nAfter a storm, algae covered the pond below the slope while wheat at the top remained yellow. Gu pointed to the total ledger: more nutrients entered than the harvest removed. The agronomist first followed the rain rather than the sum. Fertilizer washed away before crops needed it, manure leached into the ditch, topsoil eroded from the slope, and some material left soil and manure as gas.\n\nShe also sampled different parts of the fields. Some elements accumulated excessively at the foot of the slope while the crest was depleted. Other nutrients remained in the soil but in forms or seasons unavailable to wheat roots. A surplus across the whole farm did not mean every field and growth stage could obtain what it needed.\n\nThe farm began storing manure properly, composting it, and returning it according to soil results. More straw stayed on the land or found another use. Fertilizer was split across periods of actual crop demand, while cover crops and buffer strips protected the slope and kept the pond from receiving every excess. Nutrients leaving in products still required replacement, but inputs now had timing and destination.\n\nThe following spring, the first manure cart did not take its usual road toward the ditch. Standing at the junction, Gu waved a flag and sent it uphill to the poorest field.",
