    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "White Creek Orchard followed a calendar left by the owner's grandfather: hives arrived on 12 April, flowers were thinned on 20 April, and late-frost precautions began in early May. For decades the dates rarely failed. Owner He Jia printed them on the shed wall so no one needed to look up and guess the season.\n\nOne winter was unusually warm, followed by clear heat at the end of February. Pear branches filled with blossom before March ended, but the beekeeper arrived on the contracted 12 April. The hive cart entered exactly on time as white petals covered the ground. A later frost then damaged the early fruit, and autumn yield was less than half the usual crop.\n\nHe blamed the beekeeper for arriving late; the beekeeper pointed to the date in the contract. A naturalist opened years of records. Pear bloom responded to winter chilling, later warmth, and variety. Wild bees and other insects could follow different cues, while frost did not necessarily move earlier with flowering. The calendar had not been late, but dependent living events had moved apart.\n\nThe orchard began weekly standardized records of bud swelling, first and peak bloom, insect appearance, and frost dates, preserving long comparisons. Hive movement followed buds and short-term forecasts rather than the month alone. Early and late flowering resources remained at the orchard edge, reducing reliance on one delivery. Records improved prediction without removing uncertainty among varieties, sites, and years.\n\nWhen the first pear flower opened the following March, He took down the old calendar. He drew a thin line through 12 April and wrote the current date in the first box of the flowering record.",
