    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "Silver Sand Harbour named its captain of the year by landed shrimp. Qiu Hai's trawler won three years in a row, with every basket of shrimp carefully recorded. Juvenile fish, turtles, and small sharks arriving in the same net could not be sold, so crews threw them back before port. Because they never entered the market, Qiu did not consider them part of the fishery's catch.\n\nIn the fourth year, nearshore fish declined while the turtle rescue centre received more injured animals. An observer joined the boat and found that one basket of shrimp often came with several baskets of non-target life. Some animals did not survive pressure or time out of water even when returned. Looking only at shrimp left effects on nursery habitat, food relationships, and other fisheries outside the ledger.\n\nQiu feared that changing the net would remove his income. A trial began on one vessel. The net gained an opening for larger animals, while mesh and fishing depth were adjusted to the target shrimp. Times and areas where young fish and turtles gathered were temporarily avoided. The observer recorded survival after release rather than merely counting animals thrown overboard.\n\nThe changes reduced some shrimp catch but also shortened sorting and reduced net damage. Monitoring continued because avoiding one place could transfer pressure elsewhere and devices performed differently by species and sea condition. Total effort, habitat, and target-stock condition entered decisions; no one escape panel carried the whole plan.\n\nDuring one haul, a turtle pushed through the square opening and swam away. Qiu did not tell the crew to narrow it. Beneath that day's shrimp total, he drew one empty square.",
