    "explanation": "Sensitivity is the proportion of people with disease who test positive; specificity is the proportion without disease who test negative. For continuous measures, changing the threshold often raises one while lowering the other, so purpose and the consequences of misses and false alarms guide selection. Neither directly equals the probability that an individual's positive or negative result is correct, which also depends on prevalence, likelihood ratios, and pre-test probability.",
