    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "Bicycles disappeared from Central Station every week. The manager posted BE A LAW-ABIDING CITIZEN notices and police added patrols, but every event remained one generic theft in the record. A young administrator mapped time and place and found most losses after evening peak from row three, hidden by an advertising board, fitted with wheel-only racks, and steps from a rear-alley exit.\n\nThey did not turn the whole station into a fortress. They moved the advertisement, installed stands that secured bicycle frames, improved sightlines during active hours, and placed staff at high-risk times. Registration and clear marking made resale harder, while the maintenance gate changed so unauthorized users could not roll a bicycle directly into the alley.\n\nTheft from row three fell sharply, but a neighbouring station then reported an increase. The team monitored displacement and examined whether cameras or access measures harmed privacy, excluded homeless people, or created unfair stops of particular groups. Making an act harder, riskier, or less rewarding had to remain fair and accompany lawful investigation.\n\nThe station also worked with police and communities on resale networks and broader social causes because environmental changes could not replace investigation, justice, and social prevention. Measures followed a precise problem and evaluation of side effects. A fall in one offence did not count as success if another harm rose.\n\nOne rainy night, the administrator deliberately locked his old bicycle in row three. At dawn, the seat was covered in drops and the bicycle remained.",
