    "storyBody": "When the Haiyuan Express opened, advertisements said only 'thirty minutes between cities'. On her first day, accountant Suna left home at six-thirty and waited for a feeder bus that came every forty minutes to the remote station. The train reached the other city on time, two minutes after the local bus had departed. Dragging luggage to her office on foot, she spent two hours in all.\n\nThe railway said its train was punctual, the bus operator said it followed its own timetable, and the station accepted no responsibility for luggage or missed connections between separate tickets. Each segment performed correctly by itself, while the door-to-door journey broke at handoffs. Wheelchair users, night workers, families with children, and people living farther away faced larger gaps.\n\nThe cities planned origin and destination access with the trunk line. Feeder arrivals allowed reliable transfer, walking and cycling routes were continuous and safe with secure parking, and platforms connected accessibly. Tickets, real-time information, and delay responsibility were integrated where possible, while baggage and freight had clear handoffs and tracking. A disruption triggered a planned alternative instead of passenger guesswork.\n\nCoordination did not pursue the shortest transfer alone. Driver schedules, waiting cost, community traffic, affordability, environment, and diverse users entered decisions. Connections that were too tight failed in cascades after small delays; those too loose wasted time. Modes retained their strengths, while interfaces made them one journey rather than unrelated companies.\n\nMonths later, Suna arrived again on the express. As its doors opened, the feeder across the same-level platform lit its destination sign. She crossed over, and the bus doors closed behind her.",
